# Offering payment systems on PagoPA S.p.A. touchpoints

This chapter describes how a PSP can offer payments via digital payment instruments (credit or debit card, bank account, digital wallet) within the pagoPA platform through touchpoints managed directly by the pagoPA platform.

The payment methods available on pagoPA touchpoints are divided into 2 main groups:

- _Native integration with the Payment Gateway_: with particular reference to payment instruments subject to the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS), their use is “centralized” within the pagoPA platform as the payment instrument data is managed directly by the pagoPA platform through its Payment Gateway component, provided by a certified supplier of PagoPA S.p.A.. The PSP must register and provide PagoPA S.p.A. with the specific data required by each payment instrument. The payment instruments natively integrated with the Payment Gateway are:
  - Visa®
  - Mastercard®
  - Amex®
  - PayPal®
  - ApplePay®
  - GooglePay®
  - BancomatPay®
  - MyBank®
  - Satispay®
- _Integrated in redirect mode_: all payment instruments not natively integrated with the Payment Gateway, which are based on bank account debits and/or are natively offered by the individual PSP, and assessed as compatible to ensure an optimal user experience for the payer.

A prerequisite for the PSP to enable all available payment instruments mentioned in the previous points—including the redirect mode—is to integrate with the Payments Hub according to the specifications of the _single model_ and to populate the informational data catalogue via the pagoPA back office, accessible from the Reserved Area.

The payer's user experience differs depending on whether they are a:

- _guest user_: the payer enters their digital payment instrument details from scratch for each payment and then proceeds with the payment;
- _registered user_: by using SPID or CIE, the user accesses the touchpoints and can save their payment instrument (if possible) to use it for subsequent payments, minimizing interactions, reducing data entry, and managing the transaction as frictionlessly as possible.

In both scenarios, the payment process is briefly described by these points:

- the user can choose a saved payment instrument or enter the payment instrument details from scratch, using the pagoPA touchpoint interfaces;
- the user ALWAYS has the option to change the selection proposed by the pagoPA platform;
- a payment summary page is displayed;
- upon confirmation of the operation, the payment is made according to the method provided by the selected payment instrument;
- once the payment phase is complete, the pagoPA platform sends the PSP the details of the notices associated with the transaction;
- when the selected PSP commits to transferring the received amount to the Entities, an email is sent to the user containing the details of the completed payment;
- if the PSP does not accept the payment, the pagoPA platform will reverse the requested payment and notify the user of the failed payment via email.

## Integration and workflow for PSP/Payment Instrument integrated with the Payment Gateway

For card payment instruments, it is necessary to configure two stores (3DS 2.0):

- channel used for card onboarding
- channel for registered user payments

As per EU Directive 2015/2366 ("PSD2"), during each payment, it will be the Issuer's responsibility to request Strong Customer Authentication (SCA) to proceed with operations (saving the instrument or payment).

The payment operation occurs in two phases:

1. authorization and settlement;
2. optional reversal/cancellation if necessary.

<figure><img src="https://lh7-eu.googleusercontent.com/yhq9AQelf18Ot0EQ5oghqgvH31BCSrnxdsNpx4IMTRxWac5VbplAkuUjaC_FXa-Hj3hQujBbU6b310J7bTx2FvhXOT6Mi7fZTCGP0So7Euw5IXkqCVpiFoxWC7OpoX8SNnxW0PaN157cmV2XSsmhpH0" alt=""><figcaption></figcaption></figure>

- Once the payment instrument and PSP have been selected, the payer triggers the client action;
- the pagoPA platform requests verification of the amount's availability via a call to the Payment Gateway;
- the Payment Gateway returns the authorization outcome;
- the eCommerce component of the pagoPA platform aligns the Hub component;
- in case of a successful authorization, the pagoPA platform informs the PSP that the authorization was successful, using the [pspNotifyPayment](../../appendix/Primitives/psp/soap-api.md#pspnotifypayment) primitive:
  - within the _additionalPaymentInformations_ section, the identification codes and the response obtained from the Payment Gateway are enclosed so that the PSP can verify the payment operation;
- if the PSP sends a [sendPaymentOutcome](../../appendix/Primitives/psp/soap-api.md#sendpaymentoutcome) after responding with a KO to the [pspNotifyPayment](../../appendix/Primitives/psp/soap-api.md#pspnotifypayment), the Hub will respond with a KO to signal the conflicting outcome;
- subsequently, within 2 seconds, the PSP communicates the completion of the payment using the [sendPaymentOutcome](../../appendix/Primitives/psp/soap-api.md#sendpaymentoutcome) call, committing to credit the bank accounts received above;
- if the PSP sends an outcome = KO after accepting the [pspNotifyPayment](../../appendix/Primitives/psp/soap-api.md#pspnotifypayment), the Hub will respond with a KO to signal the conflicting outcome;
- the pagoPA platform records the closure of the payment and sends the receipt of the operation to the Creditor Entities, unless technical problems have occurred, in which case the pagoPA platform would send a request for reversal/cancellation of the amounts;
- if the PSP responds with a KO to the [pspNotifyPayment](../../appendix/Primitives/psp/soap-api.md#pspnotifypayment) call, the platform cancels the operation and the committed funds will be returned to the user. The PSP must not send the payment completion notification using the [sendPaymentOutcome](../../appendix/Primitives/psp/soap-api.md#sendpaymentoutcome) call.
